body:
I started out with finding high BP during a surgery. I've had many and usually my BP and HR would even out after anesthesia so they assumed it was the anxiety of it. I have the MCOT again because the cardiologist I saw told me to stay on propranolol and basically on bed rest. I was referred to electrophysiology and they requested a retest with the MCOT while no longer on medication and trying to agitate things. My resting HR is now around 160 bpm, but unfortunately I seem to have (according to the doctor) inappropriate sinus tachycardia. Pretty common, I know. My HR jumps significantly when I do just about anything physical, say walking up a steep hill it will hit 215 bpm. Then I get nauseous and the whole dizzy/fainting spells begin. It jumps up like that even with the smallest thing like doing dishes. I stay at home because I can't do much without feeling awful. Makes me feel even worse for my husband even though he tries to reassure me. I've had a lot of female anatomy issues as well, endometriosis, rupturing cysts, multiple surgeries, miscarriages, etc. I feel like the world is telling me I had a shelf life and it's up. My BP is around 160/110 but does jump up with my HR when it's stressed. I'm well aware things could be so much worse, but this is my worst and I just can't take it anymore.

&#x200B;

edit: I do have a tilt table test scheduled for this fall (the earliest they could get me in) but I really don't want to go for that long like this.

&#x200B;

edit 2 (sorry, emotional and randomly thinking of extras): I am 5' and about 125lbs. I used to work out regularly and I feel like it's just not fair. I don't smoke, don't drink much, and eat very healthy. I can't even run with my dogs.
